ACT ON CARBON DIOXIDE CAPTURE, TRANSPORT, STORAGE AND UTILIZATION

CHAPTER I GENERAL PROVISIONS

Article 1 (Purpose) This Act aims to prevent the serious effects of the climate crisis and contribute to the sustainable development of the national economy by setting out the matters necessary to develop and commercialize technologies for efficiently capturing carbon dioxide emitted by industrial activities and other sources and storing it underground or using it for industrial and everyday purposes.

Article 2 (Definitions) The terms used in this Act have the following meanings:
  1. "Capture" means collecting carbon dioxide emitted from greenhouse gas emission sources, including industrial activities, or present in the atmosphere, in containers or facilities for storage or utilization in Korea or abroad;
  2. "Transport" means moving captured carbon dioxide (meaning carbon dioxide streams under Article 10(1) of the Management of Marine Debris and Contaminated Marine Sediment Act and carbon dioxide prescribed by Presidential Decree; hereinafter the same shall apply) to a storage site or utilization business facility for storage or utilization in Korea or abroad by a means prescribed by Presidential Decree;
  3. "Storage" means injecting captured carbon dioxide into onshore or offshore geological formations in Korea or abroad and permanently isolating it from the atmosphere so that it does not leak into the atmosphere;
  4. "Utilization" means converting captured carbon dioxide into a substance useful in human life or economic activity and using it as an industrial resource, or directly using exhaust gas containing carbon dioxide;
  5. "Carbon dioxide transport pipeline" means a pipeline and related facilities for transporting captured carbon dioxide, as prescribed by Presidential Decree;
  6. "Storage site" means a place meeting the standards prescribed by Presidential Decree for storing captured carbon dioxide in onshore or offshore geological formations so that it does not leak into the atmosphere or ocean.

Article 3 (Responsibilities of the State and Other Entities)
(1) The State and local governments shall establish a statistical basis and methods for calculating carbon dioxide reductions necessary for the effective management and improvement of carbon dioxide capture, transport, storage and utilization (hereinafter referred to as "CCUS"), and shall formulate and implement policies to promote the development of related industries.
(2) The State and local governments shall endeavor to promote education and publicity necessary to improve public awareness of CCUS.
(3) Business entities engaged in CCUS shall endeavor to develop and commercialize technologies and shall actively cooperate with the Government's CCUS policies.

Article 4 (Relationship to Other Acts)
(1) Captured carbon dioxide and substances or articles produced using it (excluding by-products generated during utilization that are no longer needed for the relevant business activity) shall not constitute waste under subparagraph 1 of Article 2 of the Wastes Control Act (hereinafter referred to as "waste").
(2) Where waste is mixed with captured carbon dioxide, this Act shall not apply, and the relevant provisions of the Wastes Control Act shall apply.

CHAPTER II ESTABLISHMENT OF BASIC PLANS FOR CCUS

Article 5 (Basic Plan for CCUS)
(1) To efficiently promote CCUS, the Government shall establish and implement a basic plan for carbon dioxide capture, transport, storage and utilization (hereinafter referred to as the "basic plan") every five years.
(2) The basic plan shall include the following matters:
  1. Basic directions and objectives concerning CCUS;
  2. Domestic and international trends in CCUS and prospects for technological and industrial development;
  3. Technological research, development and commercialization concerning CCUS;
  4. Selection and management of candidate carbon dioxide storage sites under Article 14;
  5. Investment in or support for CCUS facilities;
  6. Strengthening the international competitiveness of CCUS technologies and industries;
  7. Safety management and monitoring of storage sites;
  8. Publicity concerning CCUS and improvement of public acceptance;
  9. Procurement and management of necessary financial resources;
  10. Other matters necessary for CCUS as prescribed by Presidential Decree.
(3) In establishing a basic plan, the Government shall undergo deliberation by the National Climate Crisis Response Commission under Article 15 of the Framework Act on Carbon Neutrality and Green Growth for Coping with Climate Crisis (hereinafter referred to as the "National Climate Crisis Response Commission"). The same shall apply where it intends to amend important matters of the basic plan prescribed by Presidential Decree. <Amended on Nov. 11, 2025>
(4) The Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment shall support the establishment and implementation of the basic plan. The heads of relevant central administrative agencies, local governments and public institutions (meaning public institutions under Article 4 of the Act on the Management of Public Institutions; hereinafter the same shall apply) shall cooperate to the fullest extent, including by providing materials requested by the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment. <Amended on Oct. 1, 2025>
(5) Other matters necessary for the methods and procedures for establishing and amending a basic plan shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree.

Article 6 (Implementation Plans for CCUS)
(1) The heads of relevant central administrative agencies shall annually establish and implement an implementation plan for CCUS (hereinafter referred to as an "implementation plan") in accordance with the basic plan.
(2) Upon establishing an implementation plan, the heads of relevant central administrative agencies shall submit that plan to the National Climate Crisis Response Commission together with the results of implementation of the previous year's implementation plan. <Amended on Nov. 11, 2025>
(3) Other matters necessary for procedures for establishing implementation plans and submitting implementation results shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree.

CHAPTER III INSTALLATION OF CARBON DIOXIDE CAPTURE AND TRANSPORT FACILITIES

Article 7 (Reporting Plans to Install Carbon Dioxide Capture Facilities)
(1) A person who intends to install and operate carbon dioxide capture facilities shall prepare a plan to install and operate the capture facilities (hereinafter referred to as an "installation plan") and report it to the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment. <Amended on Oct. 1, 2025>
(2) An installation plan shall include the following matters:
  1. The name of the person intending to install and operate the carbon dioxide capture facilities;
  2. The location and area of the facilities and other matters concerning the site where they are to be installed;
  3. The capacity, capture method and other matters concerning the facilities;
  4. Other matters necessary for a report under paragraph (1), as prescribed by Presidential Decree.
(3) A person who has filed a report under paragraph (1) (hereinafter referred to as a "capture business entity") shall file a report on modification if the person intends to modify any important matter of the installation plan prescribed by Presidential Decree.
(4) Before accepting a report under paragraph (1) or a report on modification under paragraph (3), the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment shall consult with the heads of relevant central administrative agencies. <Amended on Oct. 1, 2025>
(5) The Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment may provide administrative and financial support necessary for the installation and operation of capture facilities to a person who has filed a report under paragraph (1) or a report on modification under paragraph (3). <Amended on Oct. 1, 2025>
(6) Matters necessary for reports and reports on modification under paragraphs (1) and (3), consultation with the heads of relevant central administrative agencies under paragraph (4), and administrative and financial support under paragraph (5) shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree.

Article 8 (Approval of Carbon Dioxide Transport Business)
(1) A person who intends to conduct business transporting captured carbon dioxide by a means of transport under subparagraph 2 of Article 2 (hereinafter referred to as "transport business") shall obtain approval from the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment with the facilities and documents prescribed by Presidential Decree; provided, a person who intends to conduct transport business that constitutes marine cargo transport business under subparagraph 3 of Article 2 of the Marine Transportation Act shall register in advance with the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries pursuant to Article 24 of that Act. <Amended on Oct. 1, 2025>
(2) Except as provided in this Act, transport business shall be governed by other Acts, including the High-Pressure Gas Safety Control Act, the Ship Safety Act, the Ballast Water Management Act, the Marine Transportation Act, the Motor Vehicle Management Act, the Railroad Business Act, and the Railroad Safety Act.

Article 9 (Safety Management Regulations)
(1) A person who has obtained approval under the main clause of Article 8(1) and intends to install and operate a carbon dioxide transport pipeline shall establish safety management regulations including the following matters and obtain approval from the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment before installing and operating the pipeline, in order to ensure its safety. The same shall apply to any intended modification of the approved regulations. <Amended on Oct. 1, 2025>
  1. Emergency response measures in the event of leakage of captured carbon dioxide;
  2. A safety management system;
  3. Matters prescribed by Ordinance of the Ministry of Climate, Energy and Environment to ensure the safety of carbon dioxide transport pipelines.
(2) A person who has obtained approval under paragraph (1) (hereinafter referred to as a "pipeline installation and operation entity") and its personnel shall comply with the safety management regulations under paragraph (1).
(3) The Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment shall periodically inspect whether a pipeline installation and operation entity and its personnel comply with the safety management regulations under paragraph (1), as prescribed by Presidential Decree. <Amended on Oct. 1, 2025>

Article 10 (Safety Managers)
(1) A pipeline installation and operation entity shall appoint a safety manager before commencing business or using a carbon dioxide transport pipeline, as prescribed by Ordinance of the Ministry of Climate, Energy and Environment, and shall report the appointment to the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment within the period prescribed by that Ordinance from the date of appointment. <Amended on Oct. 1, 2025>
(2) If a safety manager is dismissed or retires, the pipeline installation and operation entity shall appoint another safety manager within the period prescribed by Ordinance of the Ministry of Climate, Energy and Environment from the date of dismissal or retirement. <Amended on Oct. 1, 2025>
(3) A pipeline installation and operation entity shall designate a substitute to temporarily perform a safety manager's duties in either of the following cases:
  1. The safety manager is temporarily unable to perform the duties due to travel, illness or any other reason;
  2. A successor has not been appointed after the safety manager's dismissal or retirement.
(4) The qualifications, number and scope of duties of safety managers under paragraph (1), the period during which a substitute may act under paragraph (3), and other necessary matters shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree.

Article 11 (Safety Inspections)
(1) A pipeline installation and operation entity shall undergo the following safety inspections of its carbon dioxide transport pipeline conducted by the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment: <Amended on Oct. 1, 2025>
  1. Periodic inspection: An inspection conducted every year. Its validity period shall be one year from the date of inspection, and the first periodic inspection shall be conducted within one year from the date of the completion inspection;
  2. Occasional inspection: An inspection conducted when the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment deems it necessary because a carbon dioxide leakage accident has occurred or is likely to occur during use.
(2) The Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment may establish and publicly notify matters necessary for the safety inspection standards concerning methods and procedures for inspections under paragraph (1). <Amended on Oct. 1, 2025>
(3) If the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment recognizes that a pipeline installation and operation entity cannot undergo an inspection under paragraph (1) due to a natural disaster or any other unavoidable cause, the Minister may extend the validity period of the safety inspection or defer its implementation, as prescribed by Presidential Decree. <Amended on Oct. 1, 2025>
(4) Matters necessary for conducting safety inspections under paragraph (1) shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree.

Article 12 (Entry onto Another Person's Land)
(1) A pipeline installation and operation entity may enter or use another person's land, or alter or remove plants or other obstacles on that land, if necessary for an on-site investigation, survey, construction or operation relating to the installation of a carbon dioxide transport pipeline or for its preservation.
(2) The Act on Acquisition of and Compensation for Land, etc. for Public Works Projects shall apply mutatis mutandis to entry onto and use of land, alteration or removal of plants or other obstacles, and compensation therefor under paragraph (1).

CHAPTER IV EXPLORATION, SELECTION AND CLOSURE OF CANDIDATE CARBON DIOXIDE STORAGE SITES

Article 13 (Approval for Exploration of Storage Sites)
(1) A person who intends to explore onshore or offshore geological formations to discover a storage site shall obtain approval from the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment. An application for approval may include an area for which an exploration right has been established under Article 12 of the Submarine Mineral Resources Development Act. <Amended on Oct. 1, 2025>
(2) An applicant for approval under paragraph (1) shall submit to the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment an application accompanied by the following documents: <Amended on Oct. 1, 2025>
  1. An onshore or submarine area map indicating the area to be explored;
  2. An exploration plan;
  3. Specifications of major equipment required for exploration;
  4. A plan to investigate the crustal structure, distribution of faults and seismic characteristics of the exploration area;
  5. Other documents prescribed by Presidential Decree.
(3) The Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment may approve exploration if the documents submitted under paragraph (2) meet the criteria for approval prescribed by Presidential Decree. For exploration of an offshore geological storage site, the Minister shall hear the opinions of the Minister of Trade, Industry and Resources and the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries; regarding subparagraph 4 of paragraph (2), the Minister may hear the opinion of the Administrator of the Korea Meteorological Administration. <Amended on Oct. 1, 2025>
(4) In granting approval under paragraph (3), the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment may attach conditions or obligations necessary for conducting exploration. <Amended on Oct. 1, 2025>
(5) The Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment may revoke the approval if a person approved for exploration under paragraph (1) (hereinafter referred to as an "exploration right holder") falls under either of the following subparagraphs: <Amended on Oct. 1, 2025>
  1. The person fails to fulfill a condition or obligation attached to the approval;
  2. The person violates the criteria for exploration approval under paragraph (3).
(6) An exploration right holder shall submit the results of exploration to the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment within three years from the date of approval, as prescribed by Presidential Decree. <Amended on Oct. 1, 2025>
(7) If the exploration right holder cannot submit the results within the period specified in paragraph (6) due to force majeure or falls under a circumstance prescribed by Presidential Decree, the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment may extend that period once, by no more than three years. <Amended on Oct. 1, 2025>
(8) Procedures for exploration approval under paragraph (1), procedures for revocation under paragraph (5), and other necessary matters shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree.

Article 14 (Selection of Candidate Storage Sites)
(1) The Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment and the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries may select any of the following places as a candidate carbon dioxide storage site (hereinafter referred to as a "candidate storage site"). They may first hear the opinions of the heads of relevant central administrative agencies. <Amended on Oct. 1, 2025>
  1. A place for which an exploration right holder who has submitted exploration results applies for selection;
  2. A mining area in which a mining right has expired, including on expiration of its duration under Article 12 of the Mining Industry Act;
  3. A submarine mining area in which the right to extract submarine natural gas has expired under Article 19(2) of the Submarine Mineral Resources Development Act;
  4. A place recognized as suitable as a candidate storage site on the basis of the results of a research project to discover storage sites.
(2) A person seeking selection of a place under paragraph (1) shall submit to the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment and the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries an application accompanied by the following documents: <Amended on Oct. 1, 2025>
  1. An onshore or submarine area map indicating the candidate storage site;
  2. An exploration report and an evaluation report concerning the candidate storage site;
  3. A report evaluating the safety and environmental impact of the candidate storage site;
  4. A report evaluating the suitability of its location;
  5. A report evaluating the crustal structure, distribution of faults and seismic characteristics of the exploration area;
  6. Other documents prescribed by Presidential Decree.
(3) If a site is selected as a candidate storage site upon an exploration right holder's application, that person's exploration right shall expire notwithstanding the remaining exploration period.
(4) Other matters necessary for the criteria and procedures for selecting candidate storage sites shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree.

Article 15 (Public Announcement of Candidate Storage Sites)
(1) If the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment and the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries select a candidate storage site under Article 14(1), they shall publicly announce its location and other matters prescribed by Presidential Decree. <Amended on Oct. 1, 2025>
(2) Matters necessary for the method and procedures of public announcement under paragraph (1) shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree.

Article 16 (Revocation of Selection of Candidate Storage Sites)
(1) The Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment and the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries may revoke the selection of a candidate storage site under Article 14(1) in any of the following cases: <Amended on Oct. 1, 2025>
  1. An unforeseen serious problem concerning geological characteristics and safety arises during construction related to a storage site;
  2. A serious defect in the process of granting permission for storage business under the main clause of Article 18(1) may cause serious harm to an ecosystem;
  3. A disaster makes it impossible to grant permission for storage business under the main clause of Article 18(1).
(2) If selection of a candidate storage site is revoked under paragraph (1), the Ministers shall publicly announce that fact. <Amended on Oct. 1, 2025>
(3) Matters necessary for revocation under paragraph (1) and the content and method of public announcement under paragraph (2) shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree.

Article 17 (Closure of Storage Sites)
(1) The Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment may order closure of a storage site in any of the following cases: <Amended on Oct. 1, 2025>
  1. Its storage capacity is full and further storage is impossible or ineffective;
  2. Following a request to close the site by a person permitted to conduct storage business under the main clause of Article 18(1) (hereinafter referred to as a "storage business entity"), the Minister decides to close it as prescribed by Presidential Decree;
  3. The Minister recognizes that closure is appropriate due to a natural disaster, leakage of stored carbon dioxide or another cause;
  4. Closure is necessary in any other case prescribed by Presidential Decree.
(2) The storage business entity shall bear the cost of closure under paragraph (1).
(3) Matters necessary for orders to close storage sites under paragraph (1) and the procedures and methods of closure shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree.
(4) Upon completion of closure under paragraph (1), the storage business entity shall immediately restore the facilities installed for storage business and other related facilities to their original state in accordance with the standards and procedures prescribed by Presidential Decree.
(5) A storage business entity that has completed restoration under paragraph (4) shall obtain approval from the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment as prescribed by Presidential Decree. <Amended on Oct. 1, 2025>

CHAPTER V PERMISSION FOR STORAGE BUSINESS

Article 18 (Permission for Storage Business)
(1) A person intending to conduct storage business shall obtain permission from the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment as prescribed by Presidential Decree; the same shall apply to any modification of permitted matters. However, a person intending to store carbon dioxide streams in offshore geological formations shall first obtain permission from the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries under Article 10(1) and (2) of the Management of Marine Debris and Contaminated Marine Sediment Act. <Amended on Oct. 1, 2025>
(2) An exploration right holder shall have priority when applying for permission under the main clause of paragraph (1) in respect of a storage site explored by that holder.
(3) An application for permission under the main clause of paragraph (1) shall be accompanied by documents concerning:
  1. Procedures and methods for managing captured carbon dioxide at the storage site and related facilities;
  2. Inspection, maintenance and repair of the storage site and related facilities;
  3. Management of the purity and compressed state of stored carbon dioxide;
  4. Leakage and migration of stored carbon dioxide;
  5. Disclosure of information on operation of the storage site and related facilities;
  6. Other matters deemed necessary for operation of the storage site and related facilities.
(4) The criteria for permission under the main clause of paragraph (1) are as follows, and details of those criteria shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree:
  1. Commencement or modification of storage business shall not impede prevention of disasters;
  2. The applicant shall have the financial resources and technical capabilities necessary to conduct storage business properly;
  3. The applicant shall be capable of possessing and maintaining a storage site equipped with facilities suitable for stable storage of captured carbon dioxide and subsequent management.
(5) A storage business entity shall report the use of a storage site in an area selected as a candidate storage site under Article 14(1) to the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment. <Amended on Oct. 1, 2025>
(6) Matters necessary for the procedures and methods of reporting under paragraph (5) shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree.

Article 19 (Grounds for Disqualification of Storage Business Entities) A person falling under any of the following subparagraphs may not obtain permission for storage business:
  1. A person under adult guardianship;
  2. A person declared bankrupt who has not been reinstated;
  3. A person sentenced to imprisonment without labor or a heavier actual penalty for violating Article 172, 172-2, 173, 173-2, 174 (excluding attempted violations of Article 164(1), 165 or 166(1)), or 175 (excluding persons who prepared or conspired to commit an offense under Article 164(1), 165 or 166(1)) of the Criminal Act, or for violating this Act, if two years have not elapsed since completion of execution of the sentence (including where execution is deemed completed) or exemption from execution;
  4. A person sentenced to imprisonment without labor or a heavier penalty with suspension of execution for an offense under subparagraph 3 who is still within the suspension period;
  5. A corporation whose representative falls under any of subparagraphs 1 through 4.

Article 20 (Succession to Business)
(1) A person who falls under either of the following subparagraphs and intends to succeed to the status of a storage business entity shall report to the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment as prescribed by Presidential Decree: <Amended on Oct. 1, 2025>
  1. The transferee, if a storage business entity transfers all or part of its business;
  2. The surviving or newly established corporation, if a storage business entity merges with another corporation.
(2) A person who acquires all storage facilities of a storage business entity through any of the following procedures and intends to succeed to the former entity's status shall report to the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment as prescribed by Presidential Decree: <Amended on Oct. 1, 2025>
  1. Auction under the Civil Execution Act;
  2. Liquidation under the Debtor Rehabilitation and Bankruptcy Act;
  3. Sale of seized property under the National Tax Collection Act, the Customs Act or the Local Tax Collection Act;
  4. Other procedures equivalent to those under subparagraphs 1 through 3.
(3) If a storage business entity dies, an heir intending to succeed to its status shall report to the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment within 30 days from the date of death, as prescribed by Ordinance of the Ministry of Climate, Energy and Environment. <Amended on Oct. 1, 2025>
(4) Upon receiving a report under paragraphs (1) through (3), the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment shall review it and accept it if it complies with this Act; provided, the Minister shall not accept a report if the prospective successor is disqualified under Article 19. <Amended on Oct. 1, 2025>
(5) If a report under paragraph (1) or (2) is accepted, the transferee, the surviving or newly established corporation, or the person acquiring all storage sites and related facilities shall succeed to the former storage business entity's status from the date of transfer, merger or acquisition.
(6) If a report under paragraph (3) is accepted, the heir shall succeed to the decedent's status as a storage business entity. During the period from the decedent's death until acceptance of the report, the permission granted to the decedent shall be deemed permission granted to the heir.

Article 21 (Succession to Effects of Dispositions) Where the status of a storage business entity is succeeded to under Article 20, the effect of an order under Article 23 to suspend or restrict storage business issued against the former entity (including a penalty surcharge imposed under Article 24 in lieu of an order to suspend business) shall extend to the successor for one year from the date on which the period of the disposition expires. If the disposition proceedings are pending, they may continue against the successor; provided, this shall not apply if the successor proves that, at the time of succession, the successor did not know of the disposition or violation.

Article 22 (Reporting Commencement of Business)
(1) A storage business entity intending to commence storage business shall report to the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment; provided, an entity intending to temporarily or permanently discontinue all or part of its storage business shall obtain permission from the Minister. <Amended on Oct. 1, 2025>
(2) Upon receiving a report under the main clause of paragraph (1), the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment shall review its contents and accept it if it complies with this Act. <Amended on Oct. 1, 2025>
(3) Matters necessary for the requirements and procedures for reporting and permission under paragraph (1) shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree.

Article 23 (Revocation of Permission for Storage Business)
(1) If a storage business entity falls under any of the following subparagraphs, the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment may revoke its permission or order suspension or restriction of its business for a specified period not exceeding six months; provided, the permission shall be revoked in a case under subparagraph 1 or 3: <Amended on Oct. 1, 2025>
  1. The entity obtained permission for storage business under the main clause of Article 18(1) by fraud or other improper means;
  2. The entity no longer meets the criteria for permission under Article 18(4);
  3. The entity becomes disqualified from obtaining permission under Article 19; excluding where an heir falling under subparagraphs 1 through 4 of Article 19 transfers the business to another person within six months from the decedent's death, or a corporation falling under subparagraph 5 of that Article appoints another representative within three months;
  4. The entity has failed to obtain permission under the proviso of Article 22(1);
  5. The entity has violated its obligations concerning the condition of stored carbon dioxide under Article 27.
(2) Standards for dispositions for each violation under paragraph (1) shall be prescribed by Ordinance of the Ministry of Climate, Energy and Environment, taking into account the reasons for and degree of the violation. <Amended on Oct. 1, 2025>

Article 24 (Penalty Surcharges)
(1) The Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment may impose a penalty surcharge not exceeding 30 million won in lieu of an order to suspend business under Article 23. <Amended on Oct. 1, 2025>
(2) If a person liable to pay a penalty surcharge under paragraph (1) fails to pay it by the due date, the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment shall collect it in the same manner as compulsory collection of national taxes. <Amended on Oct. 1, 2025>
(3) The amounts of penalty surcharges according to the type and degree of each violation subject to a surcharge under paragraph (1), and other necessary matters, shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree.

Article 25 (Establishment of Monitoring Plans)
(1) A storage business entity shall establish a monitoring plan concerning matters prescribed by Presidential Decree (hereinafter referred to as a "monitoring plan") and obtain approval from the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment before closing the storage site. The plan shall include monitoring for a period prescribed by Presidential Decree of at least 15 years after closure. <Amended on Oct. 1, 2025>
(2) Before approving a monitoring plan under paragraph (1), the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment shall consult with the heads of relevant central administrative agencies prescribed by Presidential Decree. <Amended on Oct. 1, 2025>
(3) A storage business entity shall regularly report to the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment on the implementation of the monitoring plan approved under paragraph (1). <Amended on Oct. 1, 2025>
(4) Matters necessary for the approval procedure under paragraph (1) and the reporting procedure under paragraph (3) shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree.

Article 26 (Prohibited Acts) A storage business entity operating a storage site shall not engage in any of the following acts: <Amended on Oct. 1, 2025>
  1. Releasing carbon dioxide stored at the site without approval from the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment;
  2. Operating in a manner inconsistent with the monitoring plan approved under Article 25;
  3. Altering a storage site or monitoring facility for a reason other than one prescribed by Presidential Decree, or failing to operate it normally without approval.

Article 27 (Condition of Carbon Dioxide to Be Stored)
(1) A storage business entity shall not allow the carbon dioxide to be stored to contain impurities prescribed by Presidential Decree.
(2) A storage business entity shall inject and store carbon dioxide in a compressed state meeting the standards prescribed by Presidential Decree.

Article 28 (Operation of Storage Sites)
(1) In operating a storage site and related facilities, a storage business entity shall take necessary measures, as prescribed by Presidential Decree, to prevent harm and disasters by ensuring that stored carbon dioxide does not leak.
(2) If a storage business entity learns that stored carbon dioxide has leaked in excess of the standards prescribed by Presidential Decree, it shall notify the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment without delay of the amount, timing and cause of the leakage and other relevant matters. <Amended on Oct. 1, 2025>
(3) Upon receiving notification of leakage under paragraph (2), the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment shall conduct an investigation as prescribed by Presidential Decree and report to the National Climate Crisis Response Commission on the cause and amount of leakage and other relevant matters. <Amended on Oct. 1 and Nov. 11, 2025>
(4) A storage business entity intending to temporarily suspend operation of a storage site shall obtain prior approval from the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment as prescribed by Presidential Decree. <Amended on Oct. 1, 2025>
(5) A storage business entity operating a storage site shall record and preserve the status of operation of its facilities and other relevant matters as prescribed by Presidential Decree.
(6) A storage business entity shall undergo an annual periodic inspection and occasional inspections conducted by the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment regarding management of the storage site. <Amended on Oct. 1, 2025>
(7) Standards, items and methods for periodic and occasional inspections under paragraph (6), and other necessary matters, shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree.

CHAPTER VI DESIGNATION AND OPERATION OF CARBON DIOXIDE CAPTURE, STORAGE AND UTILIZATION CLUSTERS

Article 29 (Designation of Carbon Dioxide Capture, Storage and Utilization Clusters)
(1) To foster the carbon dioxide capture, storage and utilization industry, the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment may, upon application by the Mayor of a Special City, Metropolitan City or Special Self-Governing City, or the Governor of a Province or Special Self-Governing Province (hereinafter referred to as a "Mayor/Do Governor"), designate as a carbon dioxide capture, storage and utilization cluster (hereinafter referred to as a "cluster") an area in which related enterprises and support facilities are concentrated and can create industrial synergies. The Minister may first hear the opinions of the heads of relevant central administrative agencies. <Amended on Oct. 1, 2025>
(2) A Mayor/Do Governor seeking designation shall establish a cluster development plan including the following matters and apply to the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment: <Amended on Oct. 1, 2025>
  1. The name, location and area of the cluster;
  2. The need for and expected effects of designation;
  3. A plan to develop the cluster;
  4. Regulatory exemptions applicable to the cluster, and their necessity and scope;
  5. Other matters necessary for an application for designation, as prescribed by Presidential Decree.
(3) An enterprise, public institution, or the head of a Si, Gun or Gu (meaning an autonomous Gu) may propose a cluster development plan containing the matters in the subparagraphs of paragraph (2) to the Mayor/Do Governor with jurisdiction over the area.
(4) Upon receiving an application under paragraph (2), the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment may designate a cluster after deliberation by the National Climate Crisis Response Commission. The same shall apply to modification of a designated cluster. <Amended on Oct. 1 and Nov. 11, 2025>
(5) If the Minister designates a cluster under paragraph (4), the Minister shall publicly notify its details as prescribed by Presidential Decree. Article 8 of the Framework Act on the Regulation of Land Use shall apply to public notification of topographical maps and related matters. <Amended on Oct. 1, 2025>
(6) Other matters necessary for applications and procedures for cluster designation shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree.

Article 30 (Support for Clusters)
(1) The Government may conduct the following projects in a cluster and support their costs, with details prescribed by Presidential Decree:
  1. Installation and operation of CCUS facilities;
  2. Installation and operation of industrial infrastructure and joint research and development infrastructure;
  3. Other matters prescribed by Presidential Decree for creating and activating clusters.
(2) Other matters necessary to support clusters, beyond those specified in paragraph (1), shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree.

Article 31 (Revocation of Cluster Designation)
(1) A Mayor/Do Governor may apply to the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment for revocation of the designation of a cluster under that Mayor/Do Governor's jurisdiction in any of the following cases: <Amended on Oct. 1, 2025>
  1. The purpose of designation cannot be achieved or is expected to become unattainable;
  2. Application of regulatory exemptions or similar measures in the cluster causes serious adverse effects;
  3. Another ground prescribed by Presidential Decree arises.
(2) Upon receiving an application under paragraph (1), the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment shall revoke the designation after deliberation by the National Climate Crisis Response Commission. <Amended on Oct. 1 and Nov. 11, 2025>
(3) Even without an application from the Mayor/Do Governor, the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment may revoke the designation after deliberation by the National Climate Crisis Response Commission if any ground in paragraph (1) exists. <Amended on Oct. 1 and Nov. 11, 2025>
(4) Revocation of designation under paragraph (2) or (3) shall be publicly notified.
(5) Other matters necessary for revocation of designation, including application methods and procedures, shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree.

Article 32 (Evaluation of Cluster Operations)
(1) The Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment may evaluate operation of a cluster periodically or from time to time. <Amended on Oct. 1, 2025>
(2) If necessary for an evaluation under paragraph (1), the Minister may request related materials from the heads of relevant central administrative agencies and the Mayor/Do Governor with jurisdiction over the cluster. They shall comply unless there is a special reason not to do so. <Amended on Oct. 1, 2025>
(3) The Minister shall finalize the evaluation results after deliberation and resolution by the National Climate Crisis Response Commission and notify the Mayor/Do Governor with jurisdiction; the Mayor/Do Governor shall take improvement measures based on the results unless there is a special reason not to do so. <Amended on Oct. 1 and Nov. 11, 2025>
(4) Matters necessary for the criteria, methods and procedures for evaluating cluster operations shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree.

CHAPTER VII FOSTERING CCUS INDUSTRIES

Article 33 (Special Provisions on Carbon Dioxide Supply)
(1) A capture business entity may supply carbon dioxide to research, experimental and demonstration facilities and business premises for carbon dioxide utilization.
(2) If the capture business entity supplying carbon dioxide under paragraph (1) is a controlled entity under Article 27 of the Framework Act on Carbon Neutrality and Green Growth for Coping with Climate Crisis or an entity subject to allocation under Article 8 of the Act on the Allocation and Trading of Greenhouse-Gas Emission Permits, its emissions may be calculated taking the amount supplied into account.
(3) Calculation of emissions under paragraphs (1) and (2) and other necessary matters shall be governed by the Act on the Allocation and Trading of Greenhouse-Gas Emission Permits.

Article 34 (Certification of Carbon Dioxide Utilization Technologies and Products)
(1) The Minister of Science and ICT and the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment may certify technologies and products utilizing captured carbon dioxide in order to promote their development and commercialization. <Amended on Oct. 1, 2025>
(2) The Ministers shall revoke certification under paragraph (1) in any of the following cases: <Amended on Oct. 1, 2025>
  1. Certification was obtained by fraud or other improper means;
  2. A serious defect makes certification inappropriate;
  3. The technology or product is recognized as infringing another person's rights, including intellectual property rights.
(3) A person certified under paragraph (1) may affix a certification mark to the relevant technology or product as prescribed by joint Ordinance of the Ministry of Science and ICT and the Ministry of Climate, Energy and Environment. <Amended on Oct. 1, 2025>
(4) No person shall affix a certification mark under paragraph (3) or a similar mark to a technology or product not certified under paragraph (1).
(5) Criteria, subjects and procedures for certification under paragraph (1), procedures for revocation under paragraph (2), and matters necessary for certification marks under paragraph (3) shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree.

Article 35 (Recognition of Specialized Carbon Dioxide Utilization Enterprises)
(1) If necessary to promote carbon dioxide utilization, the Minister of Science and ICT may recognize an enterprise meeting all the following requirements as a specialized carbon dioxide utilization enterprise and provide necessary support:
  1. Its investment in research and development related to carbon dioxide utilization technologies as a proportion of total sales meets the standard prescribed by Presidential Decree;
  2. It meets other requirements concerning technological level and managerial capability prescribed by Presidential Decree.
(2) An enterprise seeking support under this Act may apply to the Minister of Science and ICT for confirmation of whether it qualifies as a specialized carbon dioxide utilization enterprise.
(3) If an applicant under paragraph (2) qualifies, the Minister shall issue a certificate of recognition as a specialized carbon dioxide utilization enterprise with a validity period specified as prescribed by Presidential Decree.
(4) The Minister shall revoke recognition under paragraph (1) if the enterprise:
  1. Obtained recognition by fraud or other improper means;
  2. Is deemed unable to continue operating due to insolvency, closure or suspension of business;
  3. No longer meets the requirements in the subparagraphs of paragraph (1).
(5) Procedures and methods for recognition and support under paragraph (1), applications and issuance of certificates under paragraphs (2) and (3), revocation under paragraph (4), and matters necessary for investigations and follow-up management shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree.

Article 36 (Support for Research, Development and Dissemination of CCUS Technologies)
(1) The Government may formulate and implement policies to advance research, development and commercialization of CCUS technologies and provide necessary support.
(2) To improve the results of research and development concerning CCUS technologies, the Government may support joint research, including international joint research, among public institutions, corporations, organizations and universities.
(3) Other matters necessary for support for CCUS technology development shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree.

Article 37 (Implementation of Demonstration Projects)
(1) If the head of a relevant central administrative agency deems it necessary to promote the use and dissemination of technologies developed through research and development projects related to CCUS, the head may implement a demonstration project utilizing such technologies.
(2) A business entity related to CCUS or another person seeking to participate in a demonstration project under paragraph (1) shall obtain approval from the head of the relevant central administrative agency as prescribed by Presidential Decree.
(3) The head of a relevant central administrative agency may provide financial, administrative and technical support to entities approved under paragraph (2) (hereinafter referred to in this Article and Article 38 as "demonstration project participants"). Article 40 shall apply mutatis mutandis to matters necessary for such support.

Article 38 (Special Provisions for Demonstration Projects) Demonstration project participants shall be deemed to have obtained the following permits, approvals or registrations:
  1. Registration to manufacture containers, refrigerators and specified equipment under Article 5 of the High-Pressure Gas Safety Control Act;
  2. Permission to occupy and use public waters under Article 8 of the Public Waters Management and Reclamation Act and approval of an implementation plan for such occupation and use under Article 17 of that Act.

Article 39 (Subsidies and Loans)
(1) If necessary to foster CCUS industries, the Government may subsidize or lend to related business entities the following costs, as prescribed by Presidential Decree:
  1. Costs of technology development and training professional personnel to improve the safety and efficiency of CCUS business;
  2. Costs of cooperation and technology exchanges with foreign countries necessary for CCUS business;
  3. Costs of investment in and operation of CCUS facilities;
  4. Costs of investment in and operation of domestic or foreign storage sites;
  5. Other costs necessary to promote CCUS as prescribed by Presidential Decree.
(2) The Government may provide related business entities with financial support necessary to foster CCUS industries, as prescribed by Presidential Decree.

Article 40 (Investment of Financial Resources in CCUS Business) A person managing any of the following financial resources may invest or make a capital contribution to CCUS business from those resources:
  1. The special account for environmental improvement under Article 45 of the Framework Act on Environmental Policy;
  2. The Climate Response Fund under Article 69 of the Framework Act on Carbon Neutrality and Green Growth for Coping with Climate Crisis;
  3. Other funds prescribed by Presidential Decree whose establishment purpose is equivalent to that of the fund under subparagraph 2.

Article 41 (Training of Professional Personnel)
(1) The Government may train professional personnel necessary to promote the development of CCUS technologies and stimulate related industries.
(2) For training under paragraph (1), the Government may designate and manage universities, research institutes and other institutions or organizations with appropriate facilities and personnel as professional training institutions.
(3) The Government may support training costs of institutions designated under paragraph (2) within the budget as prescribed by Presidential Decree.
(4) Criteria and methods for designation of professional training institutions under paragraph (2), and other necessary matters, shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree.

Article 42 (Promotion of International Cooperation) The Government may promote international cooperation to understand international trends in CCUS and secure overseas storage sites. In doing so, it may support international exchanges of CCUS technologies and professional personnel and international joint research and development.

Article 43 (Standardization of Technology)
(1) The Government may promote standardization of CCUS technologies to improve their quality; provided, matters for which Korean Industrial Standards have been established under the Industrial Standardization Act shall comply with those standards.
(2) To promote standardization under paragraph (1), the Government may recommend that a capture business entity or storage business entity use standards for CCUS technologies.
(3) To carry out standardization efficiently, the Government may designate institutions or organizations specializing in CCUS technologies to conduct research on standardization and may support all or part of their necessary costs.
(4) Matters necessary for designation and support under paragraph (3) shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree.

Article 44 (Establishment of a Carbon Dioxide Capture, Storage and Utilization Promotion Center)
(1) To efficiently support the development of technologies and industries related to CCUS, the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment may establish an institution or corporation meeting requirements prescribed by Presidential Decree concerning professional personnel, facilities and other matters as a Carbon Dioxide Capture, Storage and Utilization Promotion Center (hereinafter referred to as the "Promotion Center"). <Amended on Oct. 1, 2025>
(2) The Promotion Center shall conduct the following projects:
  1. Research and analysis of CCUS markets and use of information collected;
  2. Support for research and development projects related to CCUS;
  3. Support for business startups and management related to CCUS, and collection and management of related information;
  4. Projects entrusted by the heads of relevant central administrative agencies to foster CCUS industries;
  5. Projects supporting discovery of new industries related to CCUS;
  6. Support for professional personnel needed by small and medium enterprises related to CCUS to develop new products and identify new industries;
  7. Other projects prescribed by Presidential Decree as necessary to develop new products or promote new industries related to CCUS.
(3) If necessary to conduct projects under paragraph (2), the Promotion Center may request universities, institutions or organizations prescribed by Presidential Decree to dispatch their researchers, experts or other personnel.
(4) The heads of relevant central administrative agencies may subsidize all or part of the costs necessary for the Promotion Center to conduct projects under paragraph (2) within the budget.
(5) The head of a university, institution or organization dispatching a researcher, expert or other person under paragraph (3) shall not disadvantage that person in status or remuneration.
(6) Other matters necessary for establishment and operation of the Promotion Center shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree.

CHAPTER VIII SUPPLEMENTARY PROVISIONS

Article 45 (Establishment and Operation of a Public Monitoring System)
(1) The heads of relevant central administrative agencies may establish and operate a public monitoring system for CCUS (hereinafter referred to as "public monitoring"), separate from the monitoring plan of a storage business entity under Article 25.
(2) The heads of relevant central administrative agencies may designate a dedicated institution (hereinafter referred to as a "dedicated institution") for establishment and operation of the public monitoring system as prescribed by Presidential Decree.
(3) Beyond the matters specified in paragraphs (1) and (2), matters necessary for establishment and operation of the public monitoring system, the contents of public monitoring result reports, and the cycle for submitting them shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree.

Article 46 (Disclosure of Monitoring Results)
(1) The Government shall disclose the results of monitoring conducted by storage business entities under Article 25 and the results of public monitoring under Article 45.
(2) A dedicated institution shall submit an annual report on the results of public monitoring to the heads of relevant central administrative agencies and the National Climate Crisis Response Commission. <Amended on Nov. 11, 2025>
(3) Matters necessary for the content and method of disclosure under paragraph (1) shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree.

Article 47 (Reporting and Inspections)
(1) If necessary for safe and efficient management of CCUS, the heads of relevant central administrative agencies may, as prescribed by Presidential Decree, require exploration right holders, pipeline installation and operation entities, storage business entities, and enterprises certified for technologies and products under Article 34 to report or submit materials. They may authorize relevant public officials to enter offices or business premises and inspect related documents, facilities, equipment and other items.
(2) An inspection plan stating its date, time, reasons and content shall be notified to the person subject to inspection at least seven days before an inspection under paragraph (1); provided, this shall not apply in an urgent case or where the purpose of inspection is deemed unattainable due to destruction of evidence or similar circumstances.
(3) An official conducting an entry and inspection under paragraph (1) shall carry identification showing the official's authority and present it to the persons concerned.

Article 48 (Orders to Take Measures against Storage Business Entities and Others)
(1) If the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment deems that an exploration right holder, pipeline installation and operation entity, storage business entity or dedicated institution may significantly impede CCUS by failing to fulfill an obligation under this Act, the Minister may order corrective action and other necessary measures within a specified period, as prescribed by Presidential Decree. <Amended on Oct. 1, 2025>
(2) Before issuing an order under paragraph (1), the Minister shall inform the relevant entity of the reasons and give it an opportunity to submit opinions and evidence; provided, in an urgent case concerning the safe management of captured carbon dioxide or storage facilities, the opportunity may be provided after issuance of the order. <Amended on Oct. 1, 2025>

Article 49 (Hearings) The heads of relevant central administrative agencies shall hold a hearing before taking any of the following dispositions:
  1. Revocation of exploration approval under Article 13(5);
  2. Revocation of selection of a candidate storage site under Article 16(1);
  3. Revocation of permission for storage business under Article 23(1);
  4. Revocation of certification of technologies and products under Article 34(2);
  5. Revocation of recognition of a specialized carbon dioxide utilization enterprise under Article 35(4).

Article 50 (Delegation of Authority and Entrustment of Duties)
(1) Part of the authority of the head of a central administrative agency under this Act may be delegated to a Mayor/Do Governor as prescribed by Presidential Decree.
(2) Part of the duties of the head of a central administrative agency under this Act may be entrusted to an institution or organization prescribed by Presidential Decree as prescribed by Presidential Decree.
(3) A Mayor/Do Governor delegated authority under paragraph (1), or an institution or organization entrusted with duties under paragraph (2), shall notify the head of the central administrative agency of the results of performance of the delegated or entrusted duties.
(4) The head of a central administrative agency may support all or part of the costs of an institution or organization performing duties entrusted under paragraph (2).

Article 51 (Deemed Public Officials for Application of Penalty Provisions) Executive officers and employees of an institution or organization entrusted with duties under Article 50(2) shall be deemed public officials for purposes of applying the penalty provisions of Articles 129 through 132 of the Criminal Act.

CHAPTER IX PENALTY PROVISIONS

Article 52 (Penalty Provisions)
(1) A person who obstructs CCUS by damaging or removing carbon dioxide capture facilities, transport facilities, storage facilities or related facilities, or impairing their functions, shall be punished by imprisonment with labor for not more than five years or a fine not exceeding 100 million won.
(2) A person falling under any of the following subparagraphs shall be punished by imprisonment without labor for not more than three years or a fine not exceeding 30 million won:
  1. A person who installs and operates capture facilities without filing a report under Article 7(1) or a report on modification under Article 7(3);
  2. A person who conducts transport business without approval under the main clause of Article 8(1);
  3. A person who installs and operates a carbon dioxide transport pipeline without approval of safety management regulations or approval of a modification under Article 9(1);
  4. A person who violates the duty to comply with safety management regulations under Article 9(2);
  5. A person who fails to appoint a safety manager in violation of Article 10(1);
  6. A person who conducts storage business without permission or permission for modification in violation of the main clause of Article 18(1);
  7. A person who commits any act prohibited under Article 26;
  8. A person who fails to take necessary measures in violation of Article 28(1).
(3) A person falling under any of the following subparagraphs shall be punished by imprisonment with labor for not more than one year or a fine not exceeding 10 million won, or both:
  1. A person who refuses, obstructs or evades a safety inspection under Article 11(1);
  2. A person who explores without approval under Article 13(1);
  3. A person who uses a storage site without reporting under Article 18(5);
  4. A person who temporarily or permanently discontinues business without permission under the proviso of Article 22(1);
  5. A person who obtains certification of technologies and products under Article 34(1) by fraud or improper means;
  6. A person who obtains recognition as a specialized carbon dioxide utilization enterprise under Article 35 by fraud or improper means;
  7. A person who fails to comply with an order under Article 48(1).
(4) A person falling under either of the following subparagraphs shall be punished by a fine not exceeding 20 million won:
  1. A person who commits an act under any of subparagraphs 1 through 4 of Article 23(1);
  2. A person who participates in a demonstration project without approval under Article 37(2).

Article 53 (Joint Penalty Provisions) If the representative of a corporation or an agent, employee or other worker of the corporation commits a violation under Article 52 in connection with the corporation's business, the corporation shall be punished by the fine prescribed in the relevant Article in addition to punishment of the violator; provided, this shall not apply if the corporation has not neglected reasonable care and supervision concerning that business to prevent the violation.

Article 54 (Administrative Fines)
(1) A person falling under any of the following subparagraphs shall be subject to an administrative fine not exceeding 30 million won:
  1. A person who commences storage business without reporting under the main clause of Article 22(1);
  2. A person who fails to obtain approval of a monitoring plan under Article 25(1);
  3. A person who affixes a certification mark or a similar mark to uncertified technologies or products in violation of Article 34(4).
(2) A person falling under any of the following subparagraphs shall be subject to an administrative fine not exceeding five million won:
  1. A person who refuses, obstructs or evades an inspection of compliance with safety management regulations under Article 9(3);
  2. A person who fails to report the appointment of a safety manager under Article 10(1), or files a false report;
  3. A person who fails to report succession to the status of a storage business entity under Article 20(1) through (3), or files a false report;
  4. A person who fails to notify leakage in violation of Article 28(2);
  5. A person who refuses, obstructs or evades an entry or inspection under Article 47(1).
(3) Administrative fines under paragraphs (1) and (2) shall be imposed and collected by the heads of relevant central administrative agencies as prescribed by Presidential Decree.

